  "full_response": "The **SOLID** principles are a set of five design principles in object-oriented software engineering that aim to make software designs more understandable, flexible, and maintainable. They were introduced by **Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob)** and are widely used in modern software development.\n\nHere’s a breakdown of each principle:\n\n---\n\n### **1. S – Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**\n**Definition:**\n*A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.*\n\n**Key Idea:**\n- A class should focus on a single functionality.\n- If a class has multiple responsibilities, changes to one may affect the others, leading to bugs.\n\n**Example:**\n❌ **Violation:**\n```java\nclass User {\n    void saveUserToDatabase(User user) { /* ... */ }\n    void sendEmail(User user) { /* ... */ }\n}\n```\n✅ **Solution (Split into two classes):**\n```java\nclass UserRepository {\n    void saveUserToDatabase(User user) { /* ... */ }\n}\n\nclass EmailService {\n    void sendEmail(User user) { /* ... */ }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. O – Open/Closed Principle (OCP)**\n**Definition:**\n*Software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be **open for extension** but **closed for modification**.*\n\n**Key Idea:**\n- You should be able to add new functionality without changing existing code.\n- Achieved using **abstraction (interfaces, abstract classes)** and **polymorphism**.\n\n**Example:**\n❌ **Violation (Modifying existing class for new shapes):**\n```java\nclass AreaCalculator {\n    double calculateArea(Object shape) {\n        if (shape instanceof Circle) {\n            return ((Circle) shape).radius * ((Circle) shape).radius * Math.PI;\n        } else if (shape instanceof Square) {\n            return ((Square) shape).side * ((Square) shape).side;\n        }\n        // Adding a new shape requires modifying this class!\n    }\n}\n```\n✅ **Solution (Using abstraction):**\n```java\ninterface Shape {\n    double calculateArea();\n}\n\nclass Circle implements Shape {\n    double radius;\n    public double calculateArea() { return radius * radius * Math.PI; }\n}\n\nclass Square implements Shape {\n    double side;\n    public double calculateArea() { return side * side; }\n}\n\n// Now, adding a new shape (e.g., Triangle) doesn't require modifying AreaCalculator.\n```\n\n---\n\n### **",
